Gretchen, Camille,<br />
<br />
Records Management picked up your escalation over the holidays. I think you=
 are looking in the wrong place, and I think the documents you want exist.<=
br />
<br />
You have been searching SENTRY, which is the examination case system. What =
did not migrate into SENTRY in 2016 was not destroyed; it was simply not co=
pied. Separately from any case system, every message sent or received on th=
e Commission's messaging system has been journaled to a write-once archive =
continuously since 2004. In September 2011 this office suspended all record=
s destruction agency-wide, which froze the journal's retention clock, and n=
othing has aged out of it since. When the agency later moved to the Capston=
e approach for electronic mail under the general records schedule, the jour=
nal archive became the retention copy of record for email, and the moratori=
um disposition instruction was carried forward into it. In plain terms: eve=
ry email any of your 2011 examiners sent or received about this examination=
 is still there, along with its attachments, in the form it was sent.<br />
<br />
The archive is not indexed by examination number, registrant or matter, whi=
ch is why nobody thinks of it. It is indexed by mailbox, date and message m=
etadata, and it is searchable by full text through the eDiscovery interface=
 Marcus's team administers.<br />
<br />
Give me the mailboxes and a date range and we can run this today. On the na=
mes in your November memorandum I would search Achterberg, Whitlatch, Okuma=
 and Raghunathan for calendar 2011 through 2013, full text, including attac=
hments.<br />
<br />
Han-Wei Loh<br />
Records Officer, Office of Records Management Services